{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>One who strikes a man and he dies.</b> Many verses were spoken in the passage of murderers, and what I am able to explain — why they all came — I will explain." "<b>One who strikes a man and he dies.</b> Why is it said? Since it is said ״וְאִישׁ כִּי יַכֶּה כָּל נֶפֶשׁ אָדָם״ (Leviticus 24), I would hear [only of] a blow without death — Scripture comes and says מַכֵּה אִישׁ וָמֵת — he is liable only for a blow of death. And if it said מַכֵּה אִישׁ and did not say וְאִישׁ כִּי יַכֶּה, I would say he is not liable unless he strikes a man — from where [do I learn] a woman or a minor? Scripture says כִּי יַכֶּה כָּל נֶפֶשׁ אָדָם — even a minor and even a woman. And further, if it said מַכֵּה אִישׁ, I would hear even a minor who struck and killed would be liable — Scripture says וְאִישׁ כִּי יַכֶּה — and not a minor who struck. And further, כִּי יַכֶּה כָּל נֶפֶשׁ אָדָם — even a stillborn might be included; Scripture says מַכֵּה אִישׁ — he is not liable unless he strikes a viable one fit to be called an אִישׁ (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:33.644885344Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}